# Hook / Service / DAO layering

```
Component → Hook → Service → DAO → Supabase / external API
```

Components may call a Service directly (skipping the hook) when no React state/effects are needed. Each layer exports through its `index.ts` barrel and is tested independently.

## Which lib?

| Data source | Package | Approach |
| --- | --- | --- |
| App's own backend (DB tables, auth, storage) | **`@helsoft/supabase-services`** (`libs/supabase-services`) | Supabase DAO — `getSupabase()` from `src/supabase/supabase-client.ts` |
| Any endpoint/store not on Supabase (AI provider, third-party REST, AsyncStorage) | **`@helsoft/services`** (`libs/services`) | External/platform DAO — `fetch` / AsyncStorage / etc. |

Same class/naming conventions in both libs. Pick the package by data source — never mix Supabase client calls into `@helsoft/services`, or non-Supabase REST/platform stores into `@helsoft/supabase-services`.

## DAO

- Supabase: `libs/supabase-services/src/dao/{feature}.dao.ts`
- REST: `libs/services/src/dao/{feature}.dao.ts`

Raw data access only. `{FeatureName}Dao`, `abstract class` with `static` methods, named export; descriptive action verbs (`getProfileById`). No business logic, no validation beyond the call itself, no React. **One DAO class per data source** (a feature can have several). Response transformation only when needed for API compatibility.

```typescript
// Pattern A — Supabase DAO (@helsoft/supabase-services)
import { getSupabase } from '../supabase/supabase-client';

export abstract class LessonsDao {
  static async getLessonsByUserId(userId: string): Promise<Lesson[]> {
    const { data, error } = await getSupabase().from('lessons').select('*').eq('user_id', userId);
    if (error) throw error;
    return data;
  }
}

// Pattern B — External API DAO (@helsoft/services)
export abstract class AiProviderDao {
  static async generateLesson(apiKey: string, prompt: string): Promise<GeneratedLessonDto> {
    const res = await fetch('https://api.some-ai-provider.com/v1/generate', {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${apiKey}`, 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
      body: JSON.stringify({ prompt }),
    });
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`AI provider request failed: ${res.status}`);
    return res.json();
  }
}
```

## Service

- Supabase: `libs/supabase-services/src/services/{feature}.service.ts`
- REST: `libs/services/src/services/{feature}.service.ts`

Business logic + validation. `{FeatureName}Service`, `abstract class` with `static` methods; instance-based (`static create()` + private `initialize()`) only when internal state is needed. Validates inputs before calling DAOs, composes multiple DAO calls, transforms data, throws clear errors. **Never fetches directly; no React.** Exported through that lib's `src/services/index.ts`.

```typescript
export abstract class ProfileService {
  static getProfile(id: number): Promise<ProfileDto> {
    if (!id) return Promise.reject(new Error('Missing id parameter'));
    return ProfileDao.getProfile(id);
  }
}
```

## Hook — `libs/hooks/src/hooks/use-{feature}.ts`

React integration wrapping a **service** (never a DAO) from either `@helsoft/services` or `@helsoft/supabase-services`. `use{FeatureName}`, `export function`, exported through `src/hooks/index.ts`. React state/effects/lifecycle live here; business logic does not (delegate to services). Data-fetching hooks use tanstack-query (`useQuery`) — add `@tanstack/react-query` to `@helsoft/hooks` when first needed (not installed yet).

```typescript
export const useGetProfile = () => {
  const { id } = useId();
  return useQuery({ queryKey: ['profile', id], queryFn: () => ProfileService.getProfile(id) });
};
```

## Types

Follow `.agents/rules/types.mdc`.

- Multi-file / shared layer shapes (DAO DTOs, service results, hook return shapes, error codes) → co-located `{feature}.types.ts` (or `@helsoft/types` for cross-lib contracts)
- Do **not** export types from the DAO / service / hook implementation file
- Private / single-file types stay next to the implementation (or inline)

## Hard boundaries

- A component never imports a DAO; a hook never calls a DAO; a service never touches React; a DAO never holds business logic.
- DTOs stay in the data layer — services map them before handing them up.
- New feature build order: DAO → Service → Hook (if needed) → barrel exports.
- Refactor direction: API calls → DAOs; business logic → services; React concerns → hooks/components.
- Supabase vs REST: choose `@helsoft/supabase-services` or `@helsoft/services` by data source (table above).

## Testing (each layer independently)

- `*.dao.test.ts` — mock `getSupabase()` (supabase-services) / `fetch` (services).
- `*.service.test.ts` — mock the DAOs.
- Hook tests (`use-*.test.ts`) — mock the services.
